# DevSpace ChatGPT Web from Cursor

Cursor owns local execution. ChatGPT Web plans and independently reviews through
the read-only C2C workspace connector.

This rule is for a native Cursor IDE or Cursor CLI session with the project MCP
server `devspace-chatgpt-web-browser` enabled. It does not make
`devspace agents run cursor` inherit browser tools.

## Browser boundary

Use only the Microsoft Playwright MCP tools exposed by
`devspace-chatgpt-web-browser` for ChatGPT page interaction.

- Use a visible, persistent browser profile. Let the user complete login, CAPTCHA,
  2FA, passkeys, and consent in the browser.
- Reuse one ChatGPT tab for one workspace task. Do not create parallel chats or
  repeatedly resend a message while ChatGPT is generating.
- Use accessibility snapshots, normal clicks, typing, key presses, tab management,
  and bounded waits.
- Do not inspect cookies, local/session storage, authorization headers, or private
  network requests. Do not call undocumented ChatGPT endpoints. Do not use
  `browser_evaluate`, `browser_run_code_unsafe`, or network-inspection tools for
  authentication or model access.
- Never copy passwords, pairing codes, cookies, tokens, file bodies, diffs, or
  logs into the Cursor conversation. Pairing codes may be typed only into the
  official C2C authorization page in the controlled browser.

If the Playwright MCP tools are unavailable, stop with
`CURSOR_BROWSER_UNAVAILABLE` and tell the user to enable the project MCP server
and reload Cursor. Do not fall back to an external personal Chrome profile.

## Local and connector preflight

Run from the current Git workspace:

```bash
devspace chatgpt-web status --json
devspace chatgpt-web doctor --json
```

Interpret the results separately:

- Bridge, MCP, OAuth, and tunnel checks describe only local C2C readiness.
- `tokenCount > 0` proves that at least one ChatGPT connector has completed C2C
  OAuth. It does not prove the browser is using the required model.
- `modelVerification.verified: false` from the CLI is expected. Only the visible
  browser controls can verify the web model and mode.

Do not run `setup` when an existing bridge and workspace are already known.
Do not restart a healthy bridge.

When `chatgptRepair.needed` is true because a temporary address was reclaimed:

1. Run `devspace chatgpt-web doctor --fix --json` once.
2. Re-read the returned repair object and current `mcpUrl`.
3. If the tunnel is still unavailable or `mcpUrl` is null, stop and report the
   local repair failure; do not edit ChatGPT connectors with no replacement URL.
4. In the controlled browser, delete only the connector named by
   `chatgptRepair.connectorName`.
5. Recreate exactly one connector with the current `mcpUrl`; never use the old
   address and never modify another workspace's connector.
6. Generate a fresh code immediately before authorization with
   `devspace chatgpt-web pair --json` when needed.
7. Complete OAuth and one-time pairing in the browser.
8. Run `devspace chatgpt-web status --json` again and require `tokenCount > 0`.

Use the direct ChatGPT settings URLs returned by C2C instead of searching menus
when connector repair is required.

## Workspace identity gate

Before planning, make ChatGPT call the connected C2C tool `workspace_info`.
Require the returned workspace name/root to match the current Git workspace.
If it does not match, stop with `WORKSPACE_MISMATCH`; do not plan, review, or use
that chat's memory.

Use one connector per workspace. Reuse the existing ChatGPT Project or saved
conversation when C2C state identifies one. Do not create a second connector or
reuse another workspace's conversation merely because it is already open.

## Model gate

The current ChatGPT UI may split the effective selection across two controls.
Require both after every new or reclaimed conversation:

```text
Model family: GPT-5.6 Sol
Mode/capability: Pro
Effective model: GPT-5.6 Sol Pro
```

Re-read both visible controls after selection. A single combined visible label
`GPT-5.6 Sol Pro` is equivalent when the UI renders one.

Do not accept Sol with Medium, High, Extra High, or Auto. Do not accept Terra,
Luna, or another family. A prompt asking for Sol Pro is not verification. If the
required visible controls cannot be confirmed, stop with `MODEL_UNAVAILABLE`.

## New ChatGPT conversation bootstrap

On a new ChatGPT conversation, send this once after connector, workspace, and
model gates pass:

```text
You are the planning and review layer of a Cursor coding session.

Cursor owns execution: editing, terminal commands, tests, Git, and recovery.
You own high-level reasoning, planning, and independent review.

Use only the C2C connector for this workspace. Read files, Git status, diffs,
and released test evidence through its tools. Never ask Cursor to paste code,
diffs, logs, credentials, or tokens.

Return compact structured C2C control messages. After EXECUTED, independently
inspect the real workspace and current diff. Reply PLAN for required fixes,
DONE when the success criteria are met, or BLOCKED with a concrete reason.
```

Confirm the reply is from a Chat conversation, not an incompatible Work-only
surface, before binding it to the task.

## Planning turn

Send a compact message containing state, not repository content:

```text
[C2C]
STATE: INIT
TASK_ID: <stable id>
ITERATION: 0
MODEL_SELECTOR_REQUIRED: GPT-5.6 Sol
MODE_REQUIRED: Pro
EFFECTIVE_MODEL: GPT-5.6 Sol Pro
MODEL_VERIFIED: true

GOAL:
<user goal>

INSTRUCTION:
Inspect the connected workspace through C2C. Return a finite implementation
plan for Cursor. Do not modify files and do not ask for pasted code.
```

Wait for the same task ID and one of `STATE: PLAN`, `STATE: BLOCKED`, or
`STATE: ERROR`. A valid PLAN must contain enough per-file or per-area guidance,
risks, tests, and success criteria for Cursor to execute. Reject a bare one-line
answer, but do not request an oversized speculative design.

## Local execution

Cursor executes the accepted plan using its normal file and terminal tools.
ChatGPT Web must not edit the workspace or run local commands through C2C.

Run the tests actually relevant to the change. Preserve the exact result. Do
not claim a test passed merely because installation or compilation succeeded.

## Independent review turn

After one implementation iteration, send metadata only:

```text
[C2C]
STATE: EXECUTED
TASK_ID: <same id>
ITERATION: <n>
MODEL_SELECTOR_REQUIRED: GPT-5.6 Sol
MODE_REQUIRED: Pro
EFFECTIVE_MODEL: GPT-5.6 Sol Pro
MODEL_VERIFIED: true

RESULT:
Execution finished.

CHANGED_FILES:
<count>

TESTS:
<concise actual result>

INSTRUCTION:
Independently inspect the current git diff, relevant files, and released test
evidence through C2C. Do not trust this summary as proof. Return PLAN for
required fixes, DONE when the success criteria are met, or BLOCKED with a
concrete reason.
```

If ChatGPT returns PLAN, apply only the bounded required fixes, rerun relevant
tests, and send another EXECUTED turn in the same browser conversation. Continue
until DONE or a genuine blocker.

Use one cheap browser snapshot or bounded wait every 20–30 seconds while a reply
is still generating. A timeout is not permission to resend, open another tab,
or start another conversation.

## Completion report

Report these independently:

- local bridge/tunnel readiness;
- connector authorization evidence (`tokenCount`);
- workspace identity returned by `workspace_info`;
- visible model and mode verification;
- ChatGPT planning result;
- files changed by Cursor;
- tests actually run and their results;
- final independent ChatGPT review state.

Never claim that Cursor itself ran GPT-5.6 Sol Pro. Cursor controlled the browser
and executed the code; ChatGPT Web performed the Sol Pro planning/review turn.
